# Elixir Koans - 02 Strings

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
```

## Strings are there to represent text

```elixir
assert "hello" == ___
```

## Values may be inserted into strings by interpolation

```elixir
assert "1 + 1 = #{1 + 1}" == ___
```

## They can be put together

```elixir
assert "hello world" == ___ <> "world"
```

## Or pulled apart into a list when needed

```elixir
assert ["hello", "world"] == String.split(___, " ")
```

## Be careful, a message may be altered

```elixir
assert String.replace("An awful day", "awful", "incredible") == ___
```

## But strings never lie about themselves

```elixir
assert true == String.contains?("An incredible day", ___)
```

## Sometimes you want just the opposite of what is given

```elixir
assert ___ == String.reverse("ananab")
```

## Other times a little cleaning is in order

```elixir
assert String.trim("  \n banana\n  ") == ___
```

## Repetition is the mother of learning

```elixir
assert String.duplicate("String", 3) == ___
```

## Strings can be louder when necessary

```elixir
assert String.upcase("listen") == ___
```

